## Experimental characterization of thermostability
Initially, specific activities of WT BsLipA and the twelve variants (Table 2) for hydrolysis of \( p \)-nitrophenyl-palmitate (\( p\mathrm{NPP} \)) were measured at temperatures between 40 and \( 60^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) after keeping them at the respective temperatures for \( 5\,\mathrm{min} \). WT BsLipA showed the highest specific activity \( (246\,\mathrm{U/mg}) \) among all BsLipA variants at the temperature of maximum activity \( T_{\mathrm{max}} \) \( (40^{\circ}\mathrm{C}) \) (S2 Fig). At temperatures above \( 55^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \), the activity begins to drop, which is probably due to an unfolding already within \( 5\,\mathrm{min} \) of preincubation. However, two variants, F58I and V96S, showed higher activities than the WT at temperatures above \( 58^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) (S2 Fig), which may originate from them being more stable at high temperatures.
Next, thermostability was assessed by measuring the activity of each BsLipA variant at temperatures between 40 and \( 60^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) after incubating the respective variant at these temperatures for \( 30\,\mathrm{min} \). Three variants, V54H, F58I, and V96S, were more thermostable than WT; they consistently showed higher activities than the WT at temperatures above \( 48^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) (Figs 3a and S3). The largest differences between thermostabilities of WT and variants of BsLipA was observed at \( 53.5^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) where the activities of V54H and V96S were twice as a high as that of the WT, and the activity of F58I was four times higher (Fig 3). The kinetic constants of these variants were derived from initial rate measurements for hydrolysis of \( p \)-nitrophenyl-decanoate (\( p\mathrm{NPD} \)) at \( 40^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) (see S1 Text). No significant impact on the Michaelis constant \( (K_{\mathrm{M}}) \) was observed, and the turnover numbers \( (k_{\mathrm{cat}}) \) were reduced by at most \( 25\% \) (Table 3). Thus, the thermostability of the variants has been increased without significantly influencing \( k_{\mathrm{cat}}/K_{\mathrm{M}} \) at \( 40^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \). Still, two of the three thermostable variants showed lower activities than WT at temperatures below \( \sim 45^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) (Fig 3). This may have been caused by a rigidification of the lipase structure in the thermostable variants (see section "Analysis of thermostability changes at the structural level" below), which may also influence the flexibility of the active site. Similarly, in a series of five orthologs of 2-deoxy-D-ribose-5-phosphate aldolase (DERA) from psychrophilic, mesophilic, and hyperthermophilic organisms investigated by us recently in terms of biochemical, structural, and rigidity properties, an anticorrelation between specific activity at temperatures \( \leq 40^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) and experimental or computed melting temperature was observed [53]. In that study, both the analysis of local rigidity by CNA and B-factor analysis of X-ray structures provided independent clues that psychrophilic DERAs have a more flexible environment of the substrate binding pocket. Thus, it may depend on the actual operating temperature of an enzymatic process whether it is worth to apply thermostable variants with increased activities at high(er) temperatures only.
Finally, the thermostability of BsLipA variants was quantified by \( T_{50}^{\prime} \) values; these values report on the temperature at which the fraction of the activity to the initial activity (at \( 40^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \)) is \( 50\% \) after incubation for \( 30\,\mathrm{min} \). This is different from the \( T_{50} \) values normally used for characterizing the thermostability of proteins [15,54,55] in that the activity here is measured at the temperature of incubation, not at room temperature after cooling. \( T_{50}^{\prime} \) thus reports on the thermo-tolerance of an enzyme during operational bioprocesses carried out at elevated temperatures for a longer duration of time, e.g., as done in the lipid processing industry [56]. The three variants V54H, F58I, and V96S showed \( T_{50}^{\prime} \) values higher by 5.7, 6.6, and \( 3.6^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \), respectively, than WT BsLipA (Fig 3c; Table 2). The predicted \( \Delta T_{\mathrm{p}} \) values for these variants were similar to each other, in agreement with the similar \( T_{50}^{\prime} \) values found, but at the lower end of all predicted \( \Delta T_{\mathrm{p}} \) (Table 2).
For the variants used as a negative control (S1 Table) [57], the thermostability was quantified by \( T_{50}^{\prime} \) values; these values report on the temperature of incubation for \( 20\,\mathrm{min} \) after which the fraction of the activity at room temperature to the initial activity is \( 50\% \). With respect to the \( T_{50}^{\prime} \) values used above, a significant and very good correlation was obtained for \( T_{50}^{\prime} \) (see S1 Text)
